\newcommand{\Q}{\mathbb{Q}}
\newcommand{\R}{\mathbb{R}}

\title{Algebraic Independence and Transcendence of\\Exponential--Logarithmic Special Values}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{30 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For real $a$ in the domain of the logarithm, define $E(a)=e^a\log(1+a)$. We separate unconditional facts about these values from conjectural transcendence claims. First, $E(a)>0$ for every $a>0$, and therefore $E(a)\ne0$. Second, if two real numbers $x$ and $y$ are algebraically independent over $\Q$, then $xy$ is transcendental over $\Q$. Consequently, algebraic independence of $e^a$ and $\log(1+a)$ is sufficient for transcendence of $E(a)$. For the concrete pair
\[
U=e^{\sqrt2}\log(1+\sqrt2),\qquad V=e^{\sqrt3}\log(1+\sqrt3),
\]
both values are unconditionally positive and nonzero. If $U$ and $V$ are algebraically independent, every rational-coefficient bivariate polynomial vanishing at $(U,V)$ is the zero polynomial. The independence hypothesis itself remains open. Separate transcendence of the factors in a product does not imply transcendence of the product, so classical theorems about exponentials and powers cannot simply be combined to settle the proposed one-variable statement. We formulate the precise conjectures, prove the established results, describe responsible numerical algorithms, and place the problem in the broader setting of Schanuel-type questions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Expressions combining exponentials and logarithms occupy a delicate part of transcendental number theory. Each operation is analytically familiar, but arithmetic classification asks whether the resulting number satisfies a polynomial equation over $\Q$. Even when classical theorems establish transcendence for individual components, sums and products can conceal algebraic cancellations.

A tempting argument for $E(a)$ is to establish that $e^a$ and $\log(1+a)$ are separately transcendental and then declare their product transcendental. The last inference is invalid: $\pi$ and $1/\pi$ are both transcendental, but their product is $1$. A valid product argument must control algebraic relations between the factors.

Our objective is to establish what follows unconditionally, isolate a sufficient independence hypothesis, and state the genuinely open inputs exactly. Numerical experiments can illustrate the results and screen bounded families of candidate relations, but finite precision cannot certify algebraic independence.

\section{Definitions and arithmetic background}

\begin{definition}
A real number $z$ is \emph{algebraic over $\Q$} if a nonzero polynomial $p(T)\in\Q[T]$ satisfies $p(z)=0$. Otherwise $z$ is \emph{transcendental over $\Q$}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Real numbers $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ are \emph{algebraically independent over $\Q$} if the following holds: for every $P\in\Q[X_1,\ldots,X_n]$, the equality $P(x_1,\ldots,x_n)=0$ implies that $P$ is the zero polynomial.
\end{definition}

For one number, algebraic independence is transcendence. For several numbers it is much stronger than coordinatewise transcendence: the pair $(\pi,1/\pi)$ has transcendental coordinates but satisfies $XY-1=0$.

\begin{definition}
Real numbers $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ are \emph{linearly independent over $\Q$} if $q_1a_1+\cdots+q_na_n=0$ with $q_i\in\Q$ implies $q_1=\cdots=q_n=0$.
\end{definition}

Linear independence excludes only linear homogeneous relations; algebraic independence excludes polynomial relations of every degree.

\begin{definition}
For $a\in\R$ with $1+a>0$, the exponential--logarithmic special value is
\[
E(a)=e^a\log(1+a),
\]
where $\log$ is the real natural logarithm.
\end{definition}

The condition $1+a>0$ is the natural real domain condition. The central conjectures concern nonzero algebraic inputs in this domain.

\section{Unconditional analytic results}

\begin{theorem}[Positivity]
If $a>0$, then $E(a)>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The exponential satisfies $e^a>0$. Since $a>0$, we have $1+a>1$, and strict monotonicity of the logarithm with $\log 1=0$ gives $\log(1+a)>0$. The product is positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nonvanishing]
If $a>0$, then $E(a)\ne0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A positive real number is nonzero.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Sign classification]
For $a>-1$,
\[
E(a)\begin{cases}<0,&-1<a<0,\\=0,&a=0,\\>0,&a>0.\end{cases}
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The factor $e^a$ is always positive. The factor $\log(1+a)$ is negative, zero, or positive as $1+a$ lies below, at, or above $1$.
\end{proof}

The sign theorem is useful as an exact consistency check for numerical evaluation, but it says nothing by itself about algebraicity.

\section{From independence to product transcendence}

\begin{theorem}[Product transcendence]
Let $x,y\in\R$. If $x$ and $y$ are algebraically independent over $\Q$, then $xy$ is transcendental over $\Q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose that $xy$ is algebraic. Choose a nonzero polynomial
\[
p(T)=\sum_{k=0}^{d}c_kT^k\in\Q[T]
\]
with $p(xy)=0$, and define
\[
Q(X,Y)=p(XY)=\sum_{k=0}^{d}c_kX^kY^k.
\]
Then $Q(x,y)=0$, so algebraic independence forces $Q=0$. The monomials $X^kY^k$ are distinct, hence all $c_k$ vanish and $p=0$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Equivalently, the substitution $\Phi:\Q[T]\to\Q[X,Y]$ given by $\Phi(p)=p(XY)$ is injective. Substitution of $X=T$ and $Y=1$ is a left inverse because $\Phi(p)(T,1)=p(T)$.

\begin{corollary}[Finite products]
If $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ are algebraically independent over $\Q$ and $n\ge1$, then $x_1\cdots x_n$ is transcendental.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A polynomial relation $p(x_1\cdots x_n)=0$ lifts to the multivariate relation $p(X_1\cdots X_n)=0$. The lifted polynomial is nonzero, as is seen by setting $X_2=\cdots=X_n=1$, contradicting independence.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Monomial consequence]
Suppose $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ are algebraically independent and $m_1,\ldots,m_n$ are nonnegative integers, not all zero. Then the number
\[
x_1^{m_1}\cdots x_n^{m_n}
\]
is transcendental.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $T=X_1^{m_1}\cdots X_n^{m_n}$ into a hypothetical nonzero annihilating polynomial. Distinct powers of the monomial have distinct exponent vectors, so the lifted polynomial is nonzero and contradicts independence.
\end{proof}

The converse need not hold. Independence is a sufficient hypothesis adapted to the substitution argument, not a necessary characterization of transcendental products.

\section{Conditional transcendence of the special values}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional special-value transcendence]
Let $a\in\R$ with $1+a>0$. If $e^a$ and $\log(1+a)$ are algebraically independent over $\Q$, then $E(a)$ is transcendental over $\Q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the product transcendence theorem with $x=e^a$ and $y=\log(1+a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{conjecture}[One-variable special-value transcendence]
If $a$ is a nonzero algebraic real and $1+a>0$, then $E(a)$ is transcendental over $\Q$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Factor independence]
If $a$ is a nonzero algebraic real and $1+a>0$, then $e^a$ and $\log(1+a)$ are algebraically independent over $\Q$.
\end{conjecture}

The factor-independence conjecture implies the one-variable conjecture, but is stronger. The restriction $a\ne0$ is necessary because $E(0)=e^0\log 1=0$.

Separate transcendence of the factors would not suffice. Merely combining the two classical theorems of Lindemann--Weierstrass and Gelfond--Schneider therefore does not prove the one-variable conjecture. One needs a theorem controlling the joint algebraic relations of the factors.

\section{The square-root pair}
Define
\[
U=E(\sqrt2)=e^{\sqrt2}\log(1+\sqrt2),\qquad
V=E(\sqrt3)=e^{\sqrt3}\log(1+\sqrt3).
\]
The inputs $\sqrt2$ and $\sqrt3$ are positive algebraic numbers and are linearly independent over $\Q$. Indeed, a nontrivial rational relation, after isolating one radical and squaring, would imply that $3/2$ is a rational square, which is impossible by prime factorization.

\begin{theorem}[Positivity of the square-root values]
The values satisfy $U>0$ and $V>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the positivity theorem to the positive inputs $\sqrt2$ and $\sqrt3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nonvanishing of the square-root values]
One has $U\ne0$ and $V\ne0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{conjecture}[Square-root pair independence]
The numbers $U$ and $V$ are algebraically independent over $\Q$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional elimination of polynomial relations]
Assume the square-root pair independence conjecture. If $P(X,Y)\in\Q[X,Y]$ and $P(U,V)=0$, then $P=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is the defining property of algebraic independence applied to $(U,V)$.
\end{proof}

The theorem makes the implication exact but does not establish its open hypothesis.

\section{Finite families and Schanuel-type expectations}

\begin{conjecture}[Finite-family special-value independence]
Let $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ be positive algebraic real numbers linearly independent over $\Q$. Then $E(a_1),\ldots,E(a_n)$ are algebraically independent over $\Q$.
\end{conjecture}

For $n=1$, linear independence means $a_1\ne0$, and the conclusion is one-variable transcendence. For $a_1=\sqrt2$ and $a_2=\sqrt3$, the statement is the square-root pair conjecture.

This expectation is Schanuel-like because it predicts algebraic independence for quantities built from exponentials at rationally independent algebraic arguments. The logarithmic factors add mixed multiplicative structure. That structure enables the substitution theorem once factor independence is known, but does not itself prevent cancellation.

A counterexample would consist of explicit linearly independent positive algebraic inputs and a nonzero rational multivariate polynomial vanishing at their associated special values. This gives the conjecture a concrete falsification criterion.

\section{Algorithms and numerical interpretation}

\subsection{High-precision evaluation}
To approximate $E(a)$, evaluate $e^a$ and $\log(1+a)$ with guard digits, multiply, and round only at the end. For positive $a$, the result must be positive. Multiprecision elementary functions can be evaluated in time quasi-linear in the requested precision up to logarithmic factors, depending on the library and argument-reduction method.

\subsection{Bounded relation screening}
Fix total degree $d$ and coefficient height $H$. Enumerate
\[
P(X,Y)=\sum_{i+j\le d}c_{ij}X^iY^j,
\qquad |c_{ij}|\le H,
\]
excluding the zero polynomial, and record the smallest $|P(U,V)|$. There are
\[
N=\frac{(d+1)(d+2)}2
\]
monomials and $(2H+1)^N-1$ coefficient vectors before normalization, so exhaustive search grows exponentially in $N$. Lattice reduction may accelerate candidate discovery but cannot turn a small residual into proof of an exact relation.

\subsection{Logical limits}
Finite precision cannot distinguish exact zero from a sufficiently small nonzero number. Furthermore, independence quantifies over polynomials of unbounded degree and height. A bounded search therefore says only that no tested candidate produced a detectable zero at the stated precision. Interval arithmetic may certify nonvanishing for a particular candidate when its evaluation interval excludes zero, but no finite list of such certificates proves independence.

Responsible reports should include precision, degree and height bounds, coefficient normalization, and residuals. They should not describe a finite search as verification of transcendence or independence.

\section{Applications and discussion}
The product theorem is a reusable bridge from multivariate independence to one-variable transcendence. Whenever a special value decomposes as a product, joint algebraic independence of the factors rules out algebraicity of that product. For the present problem this points future work toward mixed relations between $e^a$ and $\log(1+a)$ rather than isolated classifications of each term.

Five distinctions summarize the landscape:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item Positivity and nonvanishing are unconditional for positive inputs.
\item Separate transcendence of factors does not control their product.
\item Algebraic independence of factors does control their product.
\item Factor independence is stronger than product transcendence.
\item Independence of the square-root pair remains open, although its polynomial-elimination consequence is exact.
\end{enumerate}

The substitution $p(T)\mapsto p(XY)$ is elementary, but its use depends on a strong hypothesis. Separate transcendence excludes relations involving one coordinate alone; algebraic independence excludes all mixed relations. This is the structural gap in the invalid shortcut through classical transcendence results.

Potential links with periods, exponential periods, and special values should be stated cautiously. The results here provide an algebraic reduction and unconditional sign information; they do not establish unconditional transcendence for the proposed nonzero special values.

\section{Future directions}
The first target is to prove that $E(a)$ is transcendental for every nonzero algebraic real $a>-1$. The stronger factor-independence conjecture offers one route, but requires control of all mixed polynomial relations. The pair $(U,V)$ is a concrete two-variable benchmark. Progress might exclude theoretically meaningful classes of polynomial relations, establish transcendence-degree bounds under standard conjectures, or derive independence from a precise Schanuel-type assumption.

At the family level, one may ask whether rational linear independence of positive algebraic inputs propagates to algebraic independence of their special values. Intermediate goals include pairwise transcendence and independence of selected exponential and logarithmic factors.

Computational searches can reject bounded candidate families and reveal numerical patterns. Rigorous intervals can certify that individual polynomial evaluations are nonzero. Such calculations remain local, whereas algebraic independence is a universal assertion.

\section{Methodological principles}
Mixed special values should be studied in stages. Analytic domain and sign questions come first. The desired arithmetic conclusion should then be expanded into its quantified polynomial meaning. Existing theorems must be checked to see whether they control the whole expression or only its components. Finally, the weakest target conjecture should be separated from stronger sufficient hypotheses.

This discipline avoids both overclaiming and excessive pessimism. The lack of an unconditional transcendence theorem does not erase the positivity results, the general product theorem, or the exact conditional reductions. It instead locates the point at which new transcendence theory is required.

The same discipline governs computation. A bounded experiment must not be reported as an unbounded theorem. Matching numerical signs validate sampled calculations; they do not prove the sign theorem. Small residuals suggest candidates; they do not prove exact relations.

\section{Conclusion}
For $E(a)=e^a\log(1+a)$, positive inputs yield positive, nonzero values. Algebraic independence of two real factors implies transcendence of their product, so independence of $e^a$ and $\log(1+a)$ suffices for transcendence of $E(a)$. The values at $\sqrt2$ and $\sqrt3$ are positive and nonzero, while their algebraic independence remains open.

The essential contribution is the separation of unconditional theorem, conditional reduction, and conjecture. It replaces an invalid multiplication of separate transcendence statements with the exact independence hypothesis required for a valid proof and gives a precise agenda for future study of exponential--logarithmic arithmetic.

\end{document}

